# Plan 003: is the ~210 s per-task setup the offline pip fallback? (dec 022 item 2; pre-registered 2026-11-05T18:03Z)
Hypothesis: in our Kaggle timing kernel (subprocess sandbox, fresh venv), sandbox/setup.py install_editable_package's
offline `pip install --no-index --find-links --no-build-isolation -e` fails because the venv lacks the build backend
(the scorer's Dockerfile.public:9 pre-installs pdm-backend/poetry-core/hatchling/flit-core/setuptools/wheel/editables).
Its fallback `pip install --no-deps -e` (build isolation, default index, PIP_* stripped) then retries an unreachable
index until pip's default timeout/retries expire: roughly a fixed ~100-200 s.
Local repro (CPU, no quota): a fresh py3.13 venv with no backend; a minimal project with build-backend poetry.core
(as rich) and pdm.backend (as fastapi). Time (1) the offline cmd, then (2) the fallback cmd with the index pointed at
an unroutable address (10.255.255.1, connect hangs) and at an unresolvable host (fast DNS failure), and (3) the
offline cmd again after installing the backend from the wheelhouse.
Expected if true: (1) fails in a few s; (2-hang) takes ~100-210 s; (2-dns) fails in a few s; (3) succeeds in a few s.
Decision rule: (1) fails AND (2-hang) >= 90 s AND (3) succeeds fast -> "artifact of our kernel" is supported. Next:
fix the kernel (pre-install the backends from the wheelhouse into the sandbox venv, matching the Docker image; keep
setup duration/exit logged), then re-time v000 at cap 5 per dec 022 item 3. Otherwise the cause is unresolved, and
the v001 path stands. Caveat: Kaggle's offline network behavior (hang vs fast fail) is unknown, so the repro bounds
the mechanism, not the exact 210 s.
